body:
@nektro ARIA markup is intended for people with disabilities, and numerous solutions are already built around this technology. Yes, it is possible to teach an AI agent to use ARIA markup, but if it starts being used mainly for AI agents (due to growing popularity), its entire purpose would shift, ultimately harming the people who actually depend on it.

I would also like to point out that ARIA markup is somewhat excessive for AI agents. ARIA is designed to assist users with disabilities, which is why it requires detailed descriptions of significant and related elements. An AI agent, on the other hand, is not limited in this way—it primarily needs highlights of the main elements and perhaps a simple description, after which it can figure the rest out on its own. Therefore, in my view, these are two separate tasks requiring different approaches.

Open Graph seems to serve a different purpose: it highlights the content elements that matter to social networks and, for instance, provides no descriptive context crucial for LLM agents. It essentially marks only the content itself, without covering interactive elements.
